# Bound values

The bound-value extension provides one server value type, `TYPE_BOUND`, whose
named subtypes are supplied by compiled-in providers.  It is intended for
values that need native representation or integration without assigning a new
MOO type number to every extension.

The design is split into two layers. `BOUND_CORE` supplies the registry,
dispatch, ownership, reflection, and persistence machinery. `BOUND_STREAM` is
a useful provider that adds immutable `bytes` and a mutable, sealable `stream`.
A future dynamically loaded or WASM-backed provider can target the same general
model; dynamic registration is not part of this implementation yet.

## Source layout

`bound.c` and `bound.h` implement only the core extension mechanism. They do
not implement the sample MOO-visible subtypes.

`ext-bound-stream.c` is a provider extension built on that mechanism. It owns
the `bytes` and `stream` implementations and registers their `BoundTypeDef`
records. They intentionally share one provider file because `stream:seal()`
transfers its private byte store to `bytes` without copying. Future providers
such as SQLite, WASM, or Kaitai should live in their own extension sources and
depend only on the public interface in `bound.h`.

## Building

Bound support is disabled by default.  Configure it with one of:

```
./configure --enable-bound=core
./configure --enable-bound=stream
./configure --enable-bound=yes
```

`stream` implies `core`; `yes` and `all` currently select the stream provider.
A build with `--disable-bound` retains the pre-bound database version and
rejects databases using the bound-value format.

## MOO interface

There is intentionally no global `BOUND` constant.  Bound subtypes are found
by name:

```
bound_types()             => list of registered type handles
bound_type("stream")      => the stream type handle
bound_type("missing")     => E_INVARG
```

A type handle has these properties and verbs:

```
handle.name               canonical subtype name
handle.available          true when its provider is loaded
handle.owner              #0
handle.class              another handle for the same subtype
handle:new(@args)         construct an instance
handle:verbs()            instance verb names
handle:properties()       provider-defined instance property names
```

Every instance has read-only `owner` and `class` properties.  Constructors set
the owner to the calling programmer.  Provider verbs and properties marked
`BOUND_OP_PUBLIC` are callable by anyone; otherwise only the owner or a wizard
may use them.  A writable property additionally needs `BOUND_OP_WRITE`.

Bound values work with ordinary property and verb syntax, assignment,
reference counting, `tostr()`, `toliteral()`, `value_bytes()`, equality, and
`value_hash()`.  Providers may choose content equality and hashing or leave
instances identity-only.

## Provider interface

Providers declare a static `BoundTypeDef` from `bound.h` and register it during
server initialization:

```c
static const BoundTypeDef example_type = {
    "example",              /* canonical name */
    1,                      /* persistence schema */
    class_verbs,
    instance_verbs,
    properties,
    construct,
    destroy,
    encode,
    decode,
    bytes,
    equal,
    hash,
    output,
    input
};

void
register_example(void)
{
    if (!register_bound_type(&example_type))
        panic("duplicate example bound type");
}
```

`register_bound_type()` requires a nonempty name plus `encode` and `decode`
hooks, and rejects duplicate names.  Names are matched case-insensitively and
the registry retains the provider definition for the life of the server.

The main hooks are:

- `construct(arglist, progr)` constructs a MOO-visible instance.  The handler
  owns `arglist` and returns a normal built-in `package`.
- `destroy(payload)` releases provider state when the final reference dies.
- `encode(payload, encoder)` appends opaque bytes with
  `bound_encoder_write()` and records MOO graph edges with
  `bound_encoder_add_ref()`. The returned reference index can be stored in the
  provider blob format.
- `decode(schema, decoder, payload)` restores provider state from
  `bound_decoder_bytes()` and `bound_decoder_ref()`. The provider uses the
  saved schema number to migrate or reject older representations.
- `bytes(payload)` reports provider-owned memory for `value_bytes()`.
- `equal(lhs, rhs, case_matters)` and `hash(payload, stream)` provide matching
  content semantics.  If `equal` is absent, distinct instances are unequal.
- `output(...)` returns an immutable byte view plus a retention token and
  release callback.  Consumers may safely retain the view independently of
  the bound value.
- `input(...)` appends one framed connection input item to a mutable sink and
  reports the number of raw bytes accepted.  Its `binary` argument says
  whether `data` is a MOO binary-encoded input item.

The persistence ABI has two core-framed channels: an arbitrary provider byte
blob and an indexed list of core-serialized `Var` references. Providers never
receive the database stream. The decoder byte view is borrowed and valid only
during `decode`; each successful `bound_decoder_ref()` returns an owned `Var`
which the provider must eventually release with `free_var()`.

The loader consumes and validates the complete envelope before calling provider
code. It registers a new bound wrapper before reading reference sidecars, so a
sidecar can resolve to that same wrapper and ordinary database `r` records
continue to preserve aliases. Missing providers and decoder failures retain the
blob and referenced Vars opaquely for a later registration attempt.

For example, a graph-bearing provider can encode a callback edge as follows:

```c
unsigned callback_index = bound_encoder_add_ref(out, payload->callback);
if (callback_index == UINT_MAX)
    return E_QUOTA;
return bound_encoder_write(out, &callback_index, sizeof callback_index);
```

Its decoder reads and validates the stored index, then calls
`bound_decoder_ref(in, callback_index, &payload->callback)`. A real provider
should define a portable byte order and schema for its blob rather than use the
host-order integer shown in this abbreviated example.

The prototype caps each opaque blob at 64 MiB and each sidecar at 1,048,576
references. Invalid core framing fails database loading; provider-level decode
errors preserve the opaque value. Database format `DBV_Bound` introduces the
bound record; the earlier single-`Var` experiment was never assigned a shipped
database revision.

Sidecar references are strong `Var` references. This preserves reachability, but
the server remains reference-counted: a provider payload that retains itself or
a mutually cyclic bound graph will not be reclaimed automatically. A fully
WAIF-like general graph needs a cycle strategy (provider-managed weak edges,
explicit breaking, or future core tracing); persistence identity alone does not
solve runtime cycle collection.

Load-time interning remains a separate, unimplemented optimization. Distinct
immutable wrappers may eventually share provider payload storage while true
aliases and provider reference graphs retain their identities. This makes
`bytes` eligible for string-like load-time memory deduplication without making
wrapper identity part of its content contract. Mutable streams are not
eligible. See `DESIGN.Bound`.

Verb tables contain a name, handler, flags, minimum and maximum argument
counts, and up to four argument prototypes.  A maximum of `-1` means variadic.
Property handlers receive a write flag and an in/out `Var`.  Table arrays end
with an entry whose name is `NULL`.

Use `new_bound_value(type_name, owner, payload)` to wrap provider state and
`bound_payload()` to recover it in handlers.  `bound_owner()`,
`bound_type_name()`, and `bound_is_available()` expose common metadata.

### Persistence and unavailable providers

Database records contain the canonical subtype name, schema version, owner,
class/instance distinction, opaque blob, and referenced values.  Multiple references to the
same bound instance remain aliases after reload.

If a database contains a subtype whose provider is unavailable, its encoded
value is retained as an opaque bound value instead of being discarded.  Basic
identity, rendering, owner, class, and availability reflection remain usable;
provider operations return `E_INVIND`.  Registering that subtype later asks
its `decode` hook to bind existing opaque instances.

## Immutable bytes

`bytes` owns an immutable sequence of octets.  Distinct byte values compare by
content and produce matching `value_hash()` results.

Construction accepts any number of recursively encoded values:

```
b = bound_type("bytes"):new(0, 1, 255);
b = bound_type("bytes"):new("~00~01~ff");
b = bound_type("bytes"):new({0, {1, 255}});
```

Integers must be in 0..255.  Strings use the server's existing MOO binary
encoding, and lists are traversed recursively.

The instance API is:

```
b.length                    raw byte length
b:binary_string()           MOO binary-encoded string
b:string([encoding])        decode bytes as text; UTF-8 by default
```

Text conversion returns `E_INVARG` for an unknown/invalid encoding, malformed
input, or a result containing NUL, since a MOO string cannot represent NUL.

`notify(connection, b [, no_flush])` accepts a `bytes` value.  On a binary
connection it queues the exact octets.  On a nonbinary connection it queues
the same length-delimited octets followed by the protocol EOL.  Other bound
types are accepted only if they implement the immutable output-view hook.

## Mutable streams

A stream is an owner-controlled mutable byte accumulator with a default text
encoding:

```
s = bound_type("stream"):new();          /* UTF-8 */
s = bound_type("stream"):new("UTF-16LE");
```

Before this API is considered stable, the constructor is intended to grow
without changing the meaning of its existing first argument:

```moo
s = bound_type("stream"):new([encoding [, initial [, capacity]]]);
```

`initial` may be a string, stream, or bytes value. Strings are encoded using
`encoding`; stream and bytes values copy their raw octets. `capacity` is a
nonnegative initial allocation hint, not permission to exceed normal allocation
and quota limits. This expanded constructor is design work recorded in
`DESIGN.Bound`; the current implementation accepts only `encoding`.

Its read-only properties are:

```
s.length                    accumulated byte length
s.sealed                    true after seal()
```

Mutation verbs are owner/wizard-only:

```
s:append_text(value [, encoding])
s:append_binary(binary_string)
s:append_bytes(@values)
s:clear()
s:read(connection)
s:seal()
```

`append_text()` accepts strings, character-code integers, and nested lists.  It
first builds the complete encoded addition, so an encoding failure does not
partially change the stream.  The optional encoding overrides the stream's
default for that append.  It returns the number of encoded bytes appended.

`append_binary()` decodes one MOO binary string.  `append_bytes()` accepts the
same recursive integer/string/list representation as the `bytes` constructor.
Both return the raw byte count appended.  `clear()` empties an open stream.

`read(connection)` has the same blocking and framing behavior as the existing
`read()` operation.  The programmer must be a wizard or own the connection
object.  One in-band input item is appended directly to the stream; binary
connection input is decoded to raw octets, while nonbinary input is appended
as received.  The suspended call resumes with the raw byte count.  The task
queue retains the sink across the wait and releases it on input, error,
disconnect, or task cancellation.

Reading without mutation is public:

```
s:binary_string()
s:string([encoding])
```

`string()` decodes using the stream's default encoding unless one is supplied.
It has the same invalid-encoding and NUL restrictions as `bytes:string()`.

### Sealing

`s:seal()` transfers the stream's storage into an immutable `bytes` backing
store without copying it, then returns a `bytes` value sharing that store.
Every alias of the stream immediately observes `s.sealed == 1`.  A sealed
stream remains readable and persistent but all later mutation, clearing, and
input operations raise `E_INVARG`. The intended raised error should identify
that the stream is sealed rather than exposing a bare, unexplained code.

Sealing is idempotent with respect to stream state and byte content, not bound
wrapper identity. The first call transitions the backing store to immutable
storage. Each call returns a `bytes` wrapper sharing that same store without a
copy. The wrappers compare and hash by content, but callers should not rely on
them being the same bound instance, including across a database reload. Each
returned byte value keeps the stream's owner.

```moo
s = bound_type("stream"):new();
s:append_text("hello");
b = s:seal();
return {s.sealed, b.length, b:string()};
/* => {1, 5, "hello"} */
```

## Current boundary

This is the static, compile-time provider layer.  It supplies the stable value,
registry, persistence, dispatch, byte-view, and input-sink surfaces needed by
future SQLite, encoder, WASM module, or other providers.  It does not yet let
MOO code or a WASM module register a new subtype at runtime, and providers add
verbs through their type tables rather than adding new global built-ins.

See `DESIGN.Bound` for prospective native-to-MOO continuations and the SQLite
and Kaitai Struct design exercises that motivate additional provider hooks.
